Service fails after reboot|An application works after a manual start but is absent after reboot.|systemctl is-enabled APP.service|Compare enabled state, unit dependencies and previous boot logs. A service may be static and activated by another unit rather than directly enabled.|Correct the intended activation relationship or enable the appropriate unit; fix missing storage/network prerequisites instead of adding arbitrary sleeps.|Test a controlled reboot or equivalent target activation and verify the application becomes ready without manual intervention.
Bad unit override|A service stops starting after an override was added.|systemctl cat APP.service|Compare the base file and drop-ins with status and journal errors. Check command syntax, cleared/replaced directives and environment paths.|Correct the drop-in, reload unit definitions and restart only the affected service during a controlled window.|Confirm effective configuration, active state and a real application request; retain the previous override for rollback.
SSH change risks lockout|A new SSH configuration may prevent administrators from connecting.|sshd -t|Validate syntax and inspect effective authentication and listen settings. Keep an existing session and independent recovery path available.|Correct invalid settings before reloading. Apply a scoped reload and test a second connection before closing the original session.|Confirm a fresh authorized login succeeds and disallowed authentication methods remain rejected.
Cron job works only manually|A script succeeds in a shell but fails on its schedule.|systemctl list-timers --all|For the actual scheduler, inspect logs, execution user, PATH, working directory and environment. Check overlap and permissions rather than assuming the script is identical at runtime.|Use explicit paths and environment, capture output and add appropriate locking or timeout behavior. Test under the scheduled identity.|Observe a scheduled run completing with the expected output and no overlapping duplicate work.
Root disk fills with application data|The root filesystem fills even though the application normally writes to a separate data volume.|findmnt /data|Confirm whether the expected volume is actually mounted. A missing mount can leave an ordinary /data directory on root receiving writes.|Stop affected writers, preserve misplaced data, restore the correct mount and reconcile data carefully. Add a mount dependency to service startup.|Verify the source device, application data consistency and that new writes consume the intended filesystem.
Log rotation fails to reclaim space|Rotated logs disappear but storage remains full.|lsof +L1|Identify processes still writing to unlinked files and inspect the rotation policy's reopen action.|Use the application's supported reopen signal or controlled restart, then fix rotation integration. Avoid truncating unknown active files blindly.|Confirm free space recovers and new records appear in the current log after the next rotation.
Patch installed but process old|Package inventory shows a fixed version while a scanner still detects old running code.|readlink /proc/PID/exe|Compare process start time and mapped executable/library versions with the package update. Deleted mappings can indicate an old process image.|Restart affected services or reboot when required through a tested maintenance procedure; preserve availability during rolling updates.|Verify running versions and service health, then repeat the relevant detection check.
Clock-related certificate failures|Several services report certificates not yet valid or expired at the same time.|timedatectl status|Compare host time with trusted sources and check synchronization status and offset. Separate a bad clock from genuinely expired certificates.|Restore the approved synchronization service and correct its source or connectivity. Consider application effects of large clock corrections.|Confirm time offset is acceptable and certificate checks succeed without disabling validation.
Backup restoration incomplete|A restored server boots but the application cannot serve requests.|systemctl --failed --no-pager|Check restored data age, credentials, encryption keys, DNS and dependency endpoints. Verify application-level consistency rather than only archive extraction.|Restore missing dependencies or data using the documented recovery order. Keep the restored environment isolated until correctness is established.|Execute representative reads and writes, compare recovered transactions with the RPO and record total recovery time against RTO.
Fleet rollout causes widespread errors|A configuration change starts failing on the first batch of hosts.|journalctl -u APP.service --since '-15 min' --no-pager|Compare canary and unchanged hosts, rendered configuration and dependency behavior. Identify whether failure is host-specific or common to the change.|Stop further batches, roll back the affected cohort if compatible, and repair the configuration in source. Avoid concurrent manual edits across the fleet.|Verify canary recovery, rerun validation and resume only with healthy batch gates and sufficient capacity.
